                        "review": "Developed by Rafael Farias and published by Fiassink Games, FPS - Fun Puzzle Shooter offers a refreshing interpretation of the first-person shooter genre by replacing fast-paced combat with carefully designed environmental puzzles. Instead of placing players in battles against enemies, the game transforms the act of shooting into a tool for solving logical challenges. Every level encourages observation and planning, proving that an FPS can remain engaging without relying on action-heavy gameplay. This unusual direction gives the title a distinctive identity and makes it stand out among the many independent first-person games available on Steam.\n\nThe core gameplay revolves around thirty handcrafted stages that require players to hit the correct objects in the proper order to complete each puzzle. Success depends far more on understanding how the environment functions than on quick reflexes or pinpoint aim. At first glance, the mechanics appear simple, but each completed stage introduces additional ideas that gradually increase the complexity of future challenges. New interactive objects, changing puzzle conditions, and increasingly elaborate level layouts ensure that players must continually adapt their thinking instead of relying on repetitive strategies.\n\nOne of the game's strongest qualities is how it encourages experimentation without overwhelming the player. Every puzzle provides enough visual information to suggest possible solutions while still requiring logical deduction. Players are rewarded for taking time to study each environment, identify patterns, and discover how different targets interact with one another. The gradual learning curve makes solving later puzzles especially satisfying because success comes from applying knowledge accumulated throughout the campaign rather than relying on luck.\n\nThe minimalist presentation complements the gameplay well. Rather than distracting players with flashy visual effects or highly detailed environments, the graphics focus on making interactive elements easy to recognize. This clean design helps maintain clarity during puzzle solving, allowing players to concentrate on understanding each scenario instead of searching through visual clutter. The straightforward art style may not impress those looking for technical spectacle, but it serves the game's design philosophy effectively by keeping attention centered on the puzzles themselves.\n\nSound design also supports the experience by providing satisfying feedback for successful actions while maintaining a relaxed atmosphere suitable for thoughtful gameplay. Since there are no intense combat encounters or dramatic action sequences, the audio remains subtle and functional, reinforcing the game's calm pacing. This creates an experience that feels more like solving an interactive puzzle box than surviving a traditional shooter campaign.\n\nThe game's biggest strength is its originality. Few titles successfully combine first-person controls with puzzle-focused mechanics in such a direct way, and FPS - Fun Puzzle Shooter commits fully to that concept. Players expecting explosive firefights or cinematic action may initially find the slower pace surprising, but those who enjoy logic games and environmental problem-solving will likely appreciate the clever level design. While the core mechanics remain consistent throughout the campaign, the steady introduction of new puzzle ideas helps prevent the experience from becoming stale.\n\nIts niche appeal is also its primary limitation. The game offers little for players seeking competitive action, exploration, or narrative depth, and its enjoyment depends almost entirely on how much one appreciates methodical puzzle solving. Some players may also wish for additional gameplay modes or greater variety beyond the established formula. Nevertheless, within its focused design goals, the game succeeds by delivering a consistent and thoughtfully crafted experience that rewards patience and careful reasoning.\n\nOverall, FPS - Fun Puzzle Shooter demonstrates that creativity can redefine familiar genres. By transforming shooting into a problem-solving mechanic instead of a combat system, it offers an experience that feels both accessible and intellectually engaging. Although its specialized gameplay will not appeal to every audience, players looking for a unique blend of first-person interaction and logical challenges will find plenty to enjoy. Its inventive concept, progressively designed puzzles, and clear commitment to its vision make it a memorable indie title that deserves recognition for trying something different.\n\nRating: 5/10",
